==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|expediens|lang=la}} (stem ''expedient-),'' present participle of {{term|expedire||to bring forward, to dispatch, to [[expedite]]; impers. to be profitable, serviceable, advantageous, expedient}}, from {{term|ex||out}} + {{term|pes}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-expedient.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[simple|Simple]], [[easy]], or [[quick]]; [[convenient]].
#: ''Most people, faced with a decision, will choose the most '''expedient''' option.''
# Governed by self-interest, often short-term self-interest.
#*'''1861''', John Stuart Mill, ''[[s:Utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]]''
#*: But the '''Expedient''', in the sense in which it is opposed to the Right, generally means that which is '''expedient''' for the particular interest of the agent himself; as when a minister sacrifices the interests of his country to keep himself in place.
====Related terms====
* [[expede]]
* [[expedience]]
* [[expediency]]
* [[expedite]]
* [[expedition]]

